---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { project, repoUrl } from "../lib/data";
const repo = `https://github.com/${project.github_owner}/${project.slug}`;
const guides: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["docs/run_local.md", "One machine with NVIDIA GPUs", "Docker setup, data downloads, model serving, food coding and scoring."],
  ["docs/run_slurm.md", "A Slurm cluster", "Apptainer images, model jobs, food-list grading, food coding and results collection."],
  ["docs/kubernetes.md", "Kubernetes", "Generating a model-serving manifest and reviewing its settings."],
  ["docs/add_model.md", "Adding a model", "Model revisions, model weight precision, engine options and GPU allocations."],
  ["docs/pipelines.md", "Method implementations", "Inputs, outputs and interfaces for each method."],
  ["docs/ground_truth_datasets.md", "Other datasets", "Reference types and the requirements for adding a dataset."],
];
---
<Page title="Reproduce" description="Commands and guides for reproducing the benchmark from public inputs.">
  <h1>Reproduce</h1>
  <p class="lede">The benchmark code, model configurations and collected results are in{" "}
    <a href={repo}>{project.github_owner}/{project.slug}</a>. Model revisions and engine images are fixed in
    configuration files. Downloads of the SNAPMe release, FNDDS workbooks and 2023 baseline files are checked
    against recorded checksums.</p>
  <p>The harness is the program that sends photos to a model and scores its replies. A separate inference
    engine loads the model and answers requests. The example below uses Docker to run both programs on a
    machine with NVIDIA GPUs; the linked guides cover setup and food-name coding.</p>
  <p>Model runs used GPU nodes of Atlas, the SCINet high-performance computing cluster hosted at Mississippi
    State University under an agreement with USDA ARS, with NVIDIA A100 80 GB and L40S 48 GB graphics processors.</p>

  <h2 id="one-machine">One machine with NVIDIA GPUs</h2>
  <pre><code>git clone {repo}.git && cd {project.slug}
docker pull ghcr.io/{project.github_owner.toLowerCase()}/{project.slug}:latest
export SNAPME_DATA=/path/with/space          # data, model weights and runs
H="docker run --rm --user $(id -u):$(id -g) -v $SNAPME_DATA:/data -v $PWD/containers:/out \
   ghcr.io/{project.github_owner.toLowerCase()}/{project.slug}:latest"
$H fetch && $H fndds --src /data/data/fndds && $H gt --version snapme_v1
$H download --model qwen3-5-9b
$H serve-cmd --model qwen3-5-9b --gpus 1 --format compose --out /out
docker compose -f containers/compose.yaml up --abort-on-container-exit</code></pre>
  <p>The last command starts the registered engine, sends the photos and scores the replies. Food-code and
    matched-item measures require the mapping cache, built as described in the local guide. The container image
    is rebuilt for relevant changes on <code>main</code>; an image tagged with a commit identifies a specific
    build, whereas <code>latest</code> changes over time. A compatible image-and-text chat endpoint can be supplied
    with <code>snapme-eval run --base-url http://host:8000/v1 ...</code>.</p>
  <p>The main food-identification measures also require <code>snapme-eval align</code> to grade the food lists
    against the record, followed by rescoring. The Slurm guide describes grading, splitting the lists across
    jobs, and preparing a sheet for a dietitian to assess the grader.</p>

  <h2 id="guides">Guides</h2>
  <ul>{guides.map(([f, t, d]) => <li><a href={repoUrl(f)}>{t}</a>: {d}</li>)}</ul>

  <h2 id="records">Run records</h2>
  <p>A run stores raw replies, parsed food lists, timing, model configuration, prompt hash, code commit and
    generation settings. For SNAPMe, <code>SNAPME_FOLDS</code> can select fixed groups of participants; the run
    record includes this selection. The local guide describes the filter. Engine and GPU fields are recorded
    when supplied by the execution environment. Scoring uses the run, reference tables, FNDDS tables and shared
    name-to-code cache; the graded measures also use the saved food-list grades. These inputs are needed to
    reproduce a score without making new model requests.</p>
</Page>
